    <HwNote kind="grounded" label="What the green row means, and what it does not">
      <p>
        On 2026-09-20 the board came up far enough to give a U-Boot prompt on
        <code>/dev/ttyACM0</code>, and U-Boot listed <code>mmc1@91581000: 1 (SD)</code>
        and read our partitions and files off it. That is an observation, and it
        independently confirms the controller mapping: the card is MMC1, the radio
        is MMC0.
      </p>
      <p>
        Nothing on this page beyond that has been exercised. U-Boot then failed its
        own boot command &mdash; it wanted a text file named <code>force_dtb</code>
        naming the device tree, and we had written <code>force.dtb</code>, the tree
        itself &mdash; so Linux has never started on this board and no Linux driver
        on this page has ever probed.
      </p>
    </HwNote>
  </HwSection>

  <HwSection kind="subsystems" title="Which file carries which node">
    <p class="hw-lede">
      Left: the hardware and its pins. Right: where the node lives &mdash; blue for
      the SoC <code>k230.dtsi</code>, plain ink for the reference board
      <code>.dts</code>, violet for what this repository had to write.
    </p>

    <ul class="hw-key">
      <li><i class="k-soc"></i>declared in k230.dtsi</li>
      <li><i class="k-ref"></i>from the reference board .dts</li>
      <li><i class="k-ours"></i>written in nix/dts/</li>
      <li><i class="k-open"></i>not established</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="hw-rows">
      <div class="hw-row">
        <div class="hw-rail"><strong>Console</strong>CH342 ch0<br />IO38 TX · IO39 RX<br />115200 8N1</div>
        <div class="hw-mid">
          <p>
            The SoC file declares five identical <code>snps,dw-apb-uart</code> nodes,
            all disabled, and aliases <code>serial0</code> to <code>uart0</code>. The
            board file flips <code>uart0</code> on and points <code>stdout-path</code>
            at it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Only <code>uart0</code> and <code>uart3</code> are ever enabled, so the
            kernel will register <code>ttyS0</code> and <code>ttyS3</code> and there
            is no <code>ttyS1</code> &mdash; which the SDK&rsquo;s default environment
            nevertheless asks for.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="hw-nodes">
          <div class="hw-node hw-node-soc"><em>k230.dtsi</em>uart0: serial@91400000<br />status = "disabled"</div>
          <div class="hw-node hw-node-ref"><em>carried over</em>{'&uart0 { status = "okay"; }'}</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="hw-row">
        <div class="hw-rail"><strong>Display</strong>RM69A10<br />568×1232 AMOLED<br />DSI 2 lane<br />RST IO22 · BL IO25</div>
        <div class="hw-mid">
          <p>
            The whole display chain &mdash; <code>display-subsystem</code>,
            <code>vo@90840000</code> and <code>dsi@90850000</code> &mdash; is already
            <code>status = "okay"</code> in the SoC file, wired VO&nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp;DSI
            by an endpoint pair. A board file&rsquo;s only job is to hang a panel off
            <code>&amp;dsi</code> port&nbsp;1.
          </p>
          <p>
            The reference <code>-lcd</code> variant does that by including
            <code>display-st7701-480x800.dtsi</code>. Same reset pin, same enable pin,
            entirely different panel.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="hw-nodes">
          <div class="hw-node hw-node-soc"><em>k230.dtsi — already okay</em>vo@90840000<br />dsi@90850000<br />display-subsystem</div>
          <div class="hw-node hw-node-ours"><em>ours, written and compiled</em>nix/dts/display-rm69a10-568x1232.dtsi</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="hw-row">
        <div class="hw-rail"><strong>Touch</strong>GT9895 @ 0x5d<br />I²C3 on IO36/37<br />RST IO24 · INT IO23</div>
        <div class="hw-mid">
          <p>
            Our pins are <em>identical</em> to the reference LCD board&rsquo;s touch
            wiring &mdash; <code>reset-gpios = &lt;&amp;gpio0_ports 24&gt;</code>,
            <code>interrupts = &lt;23 IRQ_TYPE_EDGE_FALLING&gt;</code>, confirmed
            against the schematic. Only the chip and its address change.
          </p>
          <p>
            The node is written and inert. Mainline has never carried a GT9895: at
            v6.18 <code>goodix_berlin</code> matches only
            <code>goodix,gt9916</code>, and the binding enumerates
            <code>gt9897</code> and <code>gt9916</code> and nothing else.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="hw-nodes">
          <div class="hw-node hw-node-soc"><em>k230.dtsi</em>i2c3: i2c@91408000<br />status = "disabled"</div>
          <div class="hw-node hw-node-ref"><em>reference — different chip</em>touchscreen@38<br />compatible = "edt,edt-ft5306"</div>
          <div class="hw-node hw-node-ours"><em>ours</em>touchscreen@5d<br />compatible = "goodix,gt9895"</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="hw-row">
        <div class="hw-rail"><strong>Wi-Fi</strong>RTL8189FTV<br />SDIO on MMC0<br />EN IO45</div>
        <div class="hw-mid">
          <p>
            The schematic ties <code>WIFI_CLK/CMD/D0&ndash;D3</code> to the SoC&rsquo;s
            <strong>MMC0</strong> balls &mdash; the dedicated pads, not FPIOA pins.
            The RT-Smart board defconfig agrees:
            <code>CONFIG_REALTEK_SDIO_DEV0=y</code>.
          </p>
          <p>
            Power is a rail, not a reset line: IO45 enables an RT9080-33 LDO that makes
            <code>WIFI_3V3</code>. Our board file carries the reference&rsquo;s
            <code>&amp;mmc_sd0</code> block over unchanged and adds nothing, so nothing
            asserts IO45 under Linux either. That is a known gap, not an oversight.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="hw-nodes">
          <div class="hw-node hw-node-soc"><em>k230.dtsi</em>mmc_sd0: sdhci0@91580000<br />bus-width = &lt;8&gt;; disabled</div>
          <div class="hw-node hw-node-ref"><em>carried over verbatim</em>okay; no-1-8-v;<br />rx_delay_line = &lt;0x0d&gt;;<br />tx_delay_line = &lt;0xc0&gt;;</div>
          <div class="hw-node hw-node-open"><em>not written</em>no mmc-pwrseq, no regulator,<br />no cap-sdio-irq — unresolved</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="hw-row">
        <div class="hw-rail"><strong>Storage</strong>micro-SD<br />MMC1 on IO54–59<br />4-bit · 50 MHz</div>
        <div class="hw-mid">
          <p>
            The card is the boot device, and the one path on this page with an
            observation behind it: U-Boot enumerated it, listed its partitions and
            read our three files off the ext4.
          </p>
          <p>
            The reference settings carry over unchanged. This is the one storage path
            where our board and the CanMV v3 agree exactly.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="hw-nodes">
          <div class="hw-node hw-node-soc"><em>k230.dtsi</em>mmc_sd1: sdhci1@91581000<br />bus-width = &lt;4&gt;; disabled</div>
          <div class="hw-node hw-node-ref"><em>carried over verbatim</em>okay; no-1-8-v;<br />cap-sd-highspeed;<br />mshc_ctrl_r = &lt;0x00&gt;;</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="hw-row">
        <div class="hw-rail"><strong>USB</strong>2 × OTG<br />USB-C + host</div>
        <div class="hw-mid">
          <p>
            Two <code>canaan,k230-otg</code> controllers, both disabled in the SoC file,
            both switched on by the board file with no further properties. Nothing
            board-specific to do.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="hw-nodes">
          <div class="hw-node hw-node-soc"><em>k230.dtsi</em>usb0: usb@91500000<br />usb1: usb@91540000</div>
          <div class="hw-node hw-node-ref"><em>carried over</em>{'&usb0 { okay; } &usb1 { okay; }'}</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="hw-row">
        <div class="hw-rail"><strong>Audio</strong>Inno codec<br />I²S</div>
        <div class="hw-mid">
          <p>
            Unusually, the codec and the I²S controller are already <code>okay</code> in
            the SoC file. The board file supplies only the machine-level glue node that
            binds them into a sound card.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="hw-nodes">
          <div class="hw-node hw-node-soc"><em>k230.dtsi — already okay</em>i2s@9140f000<br />inno_codec@9140e000<br />audio@9140f400</div>
          <div class="hw-node hw-node-ref"><em>carried over</em>sound &#123; compatible =<br />"canaan,k230-audio-inno"; &#125;</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="hw-row">
        <div class="hw-rail"><strong>Accelerators</strong>KPU · GPU<br />VPU · crypto</div>
        <div class="hw-mid">
          <p>
            The NPU (<code>gnne</code> plus <code>ai2d</code>), the GC8000UL GPU, the
            video codec and the whole <code>security</code> subtree are enabled in the
            SoC file and never mentioned by any board file. They cost a new board port
            nothing &mdash; and, in the GPU&rsquo;s case, give it nothing either: no
            Mesa or Vulkan driver exists for that part.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="hw-nodes">
          <div class="hw-node hw-node-soc"><em>k230.dtsi — all okay</em>gnne@80400000 · ai2d@80400c00<br />gpu@90800000 · vpu@90400000<br />aes · hash · rsa · trng · otp</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="hw-row">
        <div class="hw-rail"><strong>Power &amp; radio</strong>BQ25896<br />BQ27220<br />SX1262 / LR2021</div>
        <div class="hw-mid">
          <p>
            Present on the board, absent from every device tree in play. The SoC file
            declares <code>i2c0</code>&ndash;<code>i2c4</code> and
            <code>spi0</code>&ndash;<code>spi2</code>, all disabled; the U-Boot pinmux
            muxes IIC0 onto IO48/49 and IIC4 onto IO7/8. Which of those the PMIC hangs
            off, and which SPI and chip-select the radio uses, is not something this
            survey established.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="hw-nodes">
          <div class="hw-node hw-node-soc"><em>k230.dtsi</em>i2c0@91405000 … i2c4@91409000<br />spi0 · spi1 · spi2 — all disabled</div>
          <div class="hw-node hw-node-open"><em>not established</em>read the schematic nets before<br />writing any of these nodes</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </HwSection>

  <HwSection kind="a trap" title="The pinmux is not in this device tree">
    <p class="hw-lede">
      Worth saying plainly, because it inverts the usual expectation:
      <code>k230.dtsi</code> and every board <code>.dts</code> in this family contain
      no <code>pinctrl</code> node, no <code>pinmux</code>, no
      <code>pinctrl-names</code>, and no regulators of any kind.
    </p>

    <p class="hw-p">
      A grep for all of those across the Linux device-tree files returns nothing. GPIO
      banks exist as <code>gpio-controller</code>s, so a driver can ask for pin 22
      &mdash; but nothing in the Linux device tree decides that pin 22 is a GPIO rather
      than <code>OSPI_D6</code>. That decision is made one stage earlier, in U-Boot&rsquo;s
      own device tree, which drives the FPIOA block at <code>0x91105000</code> through
      <code>pinctrl-single</code>:
    </p>

    <pre class="hw-code" set:html={pinmux} />

    <HwNote kind="ours" label="Consequence for the board file we write">
      <p>
        Our Linux board file must not try to set pin functions, and cannot. It inherits
        whatever FPIOA state U-Boot left behind. If a pin we need is muxed wrong, the fix
        is in the U-Boot device tree, not the kernel one &mdash; and changing it means
        touching vendored firmware.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every pin this board actually uses is already muxed correctly by
        <code>k230_canmv_v3p0.dts</code>: the AMOLED, the touch controller, both UARTs,
        the SD card and the Wi-Fi enable. <code>SEL</code> decodes against the
        alternate-function table in <code>machine_fpioa.c</code> &mdash; 0 is plain
        GPIO, 1&ndash;4 are the alternates in order.
      </p>
    </HwNote>

    <HwNote kind="undeclared" label="Two of the vendor's own comments are wrong">
      <p>
        IO23 and IO24 are commented <code>hdmi_int</code> and <code>hdmi_rset</code>,
        and on this board they are the touch controller&rsquo;s interrupt and reset.
        IO37 is commented <code>BT_UART_TXD</code> while its <code>SEL</code> value
        selects <code>IIC3_SDA</code>. The register values are the evidence; the
        comments are not.
      </p>
    </HwNote>
  </HwSection>

  <HwSection kind="numbering" title="Two GPIO banks, and the off-by-32">
    <p class="hw-lede">
      The SoC file splits 64 pins into two controllers of 32, and each numbers its own
      pins from zero. Every GPIO our board file references lands in bank 0, so the trap
      never fires &mdash; the Wi-Fi enable would be the first thing to cross into bank 1,
      and nothing references it yet.
    </p>

