    <HwNote kind="unverified" label="Still open: which UART Linux will print on">
      <p>
        The SDK&rsquo;s default environment sets
        <code>console_port=console=ttyS1,115200</code>. With these device trees
        <code>uart1</code> is never enabled and there is no <code>ttyS1</code> to attach
        to; the aliases give <code>serial0 = &amp;uart0</code> and
        <code>serial3 = &amp;uart3</code>, so our board file asks for
        <code>serial0:115200n8</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is reasoning from the device tree, not an observation. U-Boot has printed on
        this board, but Linux has not. A console on the wrong UART is indistinguishable
        from a board that did not boot, so try <code>ttyS0</code> first and
        <code>ttyS3</code> second before suspecting the image.
      </p>
    </HwNote>
  </HwSection>

  <HwSection kind="divergence" title="Where the reference board is not our board">
    <HwNote kind="ours" label="Panel — the reason a new board file exists at all">
      <p>
        The CanMV v3 LCD reference is an ST7701 480×800 RGB LCD at 39.6 MHz over a
        600×1100 total frame, which is 60 Hz. Ours is an RM69A10 568×1232 AMOLED, also
        2 lanes, also 39.6 MHz &mdash; but over a 788×1268 total frame, which is about
        <strong>39.6 Hz</strong>, not the 60 the vendor&rsquo;s own enum name claims.
        Same reset pin, same backlight pin, different everything else.
      </p>
    </HwNote>

    <HwNote kind="undeclared" label="Touch — right pins, wrong chip, and no driver anywhere">
      <p>
        Reference: <code>edt,edt-ft5306</code> at 0x38. Ours: GT9895 at 0x5d. An earlier
        version of this note said the GT9895 was &ldquo;in mainline since about 6.7, so
        this is a backport&rdquo;. Both halves were wrong.
        <code>goodix_berlin</code> landed in v6.9, and as of <strong>v6.18</strong> its
        match table carries one entry, <code>goodix,gt9916</code>. Backporting the driver
        as written would not make this touch panel work.
      </p>
      <p>
        What we do have is LilyGO&rsquo;s own GT9895 driver for RT-Smart &mdash; not a
        Linux driver, but a working register-level description of this exact part on this
        exact board. The cheapest next step is to claim <code>goodix,gt9916</code> and
        see whether the Berlin programming model is close enough; that is an experiment,
        not a plan.
      </p>
    </HwNote>

    <HwNote kind="ours" label="HDMI — a collision we sidestepped rather than solved">
      <p>
        The base <code>k230-canmv-v3.dts</code> puts an LT9611 bridge on
        <code>&amp;i2c3</code> with <code>reset-gpios = &lt;&amp;gpio0_ports 24&gt;</code>
        and <code>interrupts = &lt;23 …&gt;</code> &mdash; our touch controller&rsquo;s
        reset and interrupt. The I²C addresses do not collide, 0x3b against 0x5d; the
        GPIOs do.
      </p>
      <p>
        Our board file derives from the <code>-lcd</code> variant, which has no LT9611,
        so there is no collision in the tree we ship. Anyone merging HDMI support back in
        has to resolve it, and the schematic around that shared section has still not been
        read.
      </p>
    </HwNote>

    <HwNote kind="ours" label="Wi-Fi — on the other controller from what we first believed">
      <p>
        Earlier notes in this repository put the RTL8189FTV on MMC1 at GPIO26&ndash;31.
        The schematic ties it to the <strong>MMC0</strong> balls, the board&rsquo;s
        RT-Smart defconfig selects <code>REALTEK_SDIO_DEV0</code>, and the U-Boot pinmux
        leaves IO26&ndash;31 as plain GPIO routed to the expansion header. U-Boot on the
        hardware then listed the card as <code>mmc1</code>, which settles it. MMC1 is the
        SD card.
      </p>
    </HwNote>

    <HwNote kind="unverified" label="One CPU in the device tree, two in the chip">
      <p>
        <code>k230.dtsi</code> declares a single <code>cpu@0</code>. The K230 has two C908
        cores; the second is the one RT-Smart runs on in the vendor&rsquo;s AMP split.
        Whether Linux can or should be given the second core is not answered by any file
        read here.
      </p>
    </HwNote>
  </HwSection>

